---
import { evidenceUrl, url } from "../lib/specs";
const milestones = [
  { status: "Verified on hardware", title: "The ESP SDR demo works", text: "The user-operated live viewer delivered 2,706 verified frames over 60 seconds. Two earlier minutes also passed. Each restored and verified the original 4 MiB firmware. The localhost viewer closes before restoration.", proof: "docs/evidence/esp-demo-play-001/README.md", label: "Interactive demo and restoration", kind: "verified" },
  { status: "Fresh reception reproduced", title: "An owned BLE packet decoded", text: "Matched ten-bit/manual48 controls reproduced a complete owned BLE packet. The later control ladder retained null results and one short-read failure; full original restorations pass. Independent replay of 1,256 saved waveforms preserves both known packets after exact upper-eight-bit reduction. That supports the decoder and digital conversion, while live detection reliability and missed-event rates remain unproved.", proof: "docs/evidence/ble-bluez-control-004/README.md", label: "Current ladder control and measured plot", kind: "verified" },
  { status: "Source verified · receiver preparation reviewed", title: "Primary-header receiver trial is next", text: "The zero-data source profile passes independent audit with controller count100; that is not an air-emission count. The separate receiver preparation passes 161 host tests and seven peer groups. Root transfer review binds 69 inputs, 11 runtime paths and all 301 content-verified Nix paths. Read-only device, backup, controller and image checks pass. The operator-holder review found finalization and safety-lock defects. Corrections and fresh review must pass before the fixed three-repeat trial.", proof: "docs/evidence/ble-primary-receiver-root-preparation/README.md", label: "Root preparation and remaining launch gates", kind: "partial" },
  { status: "Verified on hardware", title: "RTL-SDR delivers useful reception", text: "The attached dipole received FM/RDS identifying WXJC. The receiver comparison is accepted; calibrated shared-signal sensitivity remains deferred.", proof: "docs/evidence/rtl-dipole-rds/README.md", label: "Current-dipole RDS proof", kind: "verified" },
  { status: "Whole host lifecycle reviewed · hardware gated", title: "Forgix builds and recovery preparation pass", text: "Attempt004 passes modeled internal 32 MHz timing. The RP stream uses 220,200 SRAM bytes and passes independent artifact/startup review. Synthetic lifecycle, runtime and factory-return preparation pass scoped host review. Register review found qualification and finalization defects. Corrections, fresh review and a current-source ARM artifact are required. Both hardware registries remain empty. Physical FPGA grade, actual clock, voltage, pin timing and loading remain unqualified; no FPGA was programmed.", proof: "docs/evidence/forgix-synthetic-lifecycle/README.md", label: "Lifecycle review, retained failure and physical gates", kind: "prepared" },
  { status: "64 KiB/s passes · 256 KiB/s loses data", title: "Forgix USB loss is measured", text: "The 64 KiB/s condition passes without loss. At requested 256 KiB/s, 15,530,520 payload bytes arrived over 60 seconds and 431 records were discarded, all accounted for. Full original-flash and factory verification passed. Maximum capacity and FPGA transport remain open.", proof: "docs/evidence/forgix-usb-ram-recovery/README.md", label: "USB measurements and retained failures", kind: "partial" },
];
---
<section class="project-checkpoint" aria-labelledby="checkpoint-title" data-project-checkpoint>
  <header><p class="eyebrow">CURRENT CHECKPOINT · OCTOBER 4, 2026</p><h2 id="checkpoint-title">The demo works. The next experiments have clear gates.</h2><p>Hardware results, software preparation and failed experiments are shown separately. The full RF and FPGA evaluations remain open.</p></header>
  <div class="milestones">{milestones.map(item => <article class={`milestone ${item.kind}`}><span class="milestone-status">{item.status}</span><h3>{item.title}</h3><p>{item.text}</p><a href={evidenceUrl(item.proof)}>{item.label} →</a></article>)}</div>
  <div class="next-work"><div><p class="eyebrow">NEXT, IN ORDER</p><ol><li><strong>Qualify the physical FPGA route.</strong> Correct the register qualification/finalization defects, pass independent review and build a fresh ARM artifact. Re-establish the matching attachment and qualify FPGA grade, actual clock, voltage and pin timing. Refresh preservation before bounded configuration/register readback, then measure the finite FPGA sequence at several rates. Host and modeled timing results do not replace these physical gates.</li><li><strong>Advance controlled ESP reception.</strong> Source profile and receiver preparation pass review. Correct the outer holder’s deadline, receipt and safety-lock defects; pass independent review and make a fresh final input snapshot, then run the fixed three ten-bit/BW20/manual48 ON/OFF pairs with verified preservation and restoration. Low snapshot duty limits any null result. Original Trial B and counted-emission requirements remain open.</li><li><strong>Diagnose USB stalls and buffering.</strong> Use retained queue and loss timing before declaring another condition. The 256 KiB/s loss does not establish a USB ceiling; the separate finite FPGA FIFO/source and autonomous RP/host path now pass offline preparation. Qualify and measure that physical route before RF integration.</li></ol></div><aside><h3>What we need from you</h3><p>The latest USB survey finds the ESP and RTL-SDR, but no Forgix matching its preserved identity. Earlier full preservation and factory return remain valid historical evidence. A matching attachment is needed for further FPGA measurements; no ESP wiring is needed for the first internal register test. Your photos are received and your S31 remains backordered. The photos show the nominal 32 MHz oscillator marking. The small FPGA chip’s T8F49 suffix is still unreadable; grade, actual clock and pin timing need qualification.</p><h3>Will Forgix need wires to the ESP?</h3><p>The proposed direct path needs verified SPI I/O and a common ground. Forgix keeps USB to the PC. A PC-mediated processing demo is another option, but keeps ESP capture gaps. No inter-board wiring is needed for the current USB test.</p><a href={url("fpga/")}>Transport stages and pin ownership →</a></aside></div>
</section>
<style>
  .project-checkpoint { margin:2.5rem 0; padding:1.6rem; border:1px solid var(--rule); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface); color:var(--ink); }
  header { max-width:64rem; margin-bottom:1.4rem; }
  h2 { font-size:clamp(1.5rem,2.7vw,2.2rem); line-height:1.15; margin:.5rem 0 .8rem; }
  header p, article p, aside p, li { color:var(--ink-2); line-height:1.55; }
  .milestones { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr)); gap:1rem; }
  .milestone { padding:1.1rem; border:1px solid var(--rule); border-top:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:7px; }
  .partial { border-top-color:var(--unverified); }
  .prepared { border-top-color:var(--muted); }
  .milestone-status { font: .65rem var(--mono); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.05em; color:var(--muted); }
  h3 { font-size:1.08rem; line-height:1.25; margin:.65rem 0; }
  article p { font-size:.9rem; margin:.7rem 0 1rem; }
  a { color:var(--accent); font-size:.88rem; }
  .next-work { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1.45fr 1fr; gap:2rem; margin-top:1.5rem; padding-top:1.4rem; border-top:1px solid var(--rule); }
  ol { padding-left:1.2rem; margin:.7rem 0 0; }
  li { margin:.65rem 0; font-size:.92rem; }
  aside { padding:1rem 1.2rem; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:7px; }
  aside h3:first-child { margin-top:0; }
  aside p { font-size:.9rem; }
  @media(max-width:1050px) { .milestones { grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr)); } }
  @media(max-width:650px) { .project-checkpoint { padding:1rem; } .milestones,.next-work { grid-template-columns:1fr; } .next-work { gap:1rem; } }
</style>
